  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a block and tackle?",
      "a": "It is a pulley system with two blocks, each holding one or more wheels, and a rope threaded between them. The bottom block moves with the load.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a pulley block?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do more rope strands make it easier?",
      "a": "The load hangs on all the strands that support the bottom block, and each carries the same tension. With four strands, each carries a quarter of the load, so you pull a quarter as hard.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you count the strands?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do I pull so much rope with a pulley system?",
      "a": "Work in equals work out. If your force is a quarter of the load, you must pull four times as far: 4 metres of rope to lift the load 1 metre.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does a pulley system need lots of rope?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is the real force exactly a quarter?",
      "a": "No. Friction in the wheels and the weight of the bottom block mean you pull a bit more. The real mechanical advantage is always a little less than the number of strands.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is a pulley system not perfect?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where are blocks and tackles used?",
      "a": "On cranes, sailing ships, in theatres, car workshops, rescue teams and gyms. Archimedes is said to have pulled a whole ship with a compound pulley.",
      "asks": [
        "Who uses pulley systems?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The ideal mechanical advantage of a block and tackle equals the number of rope strands supporting the moving block.",
    "With a mechanical advantage of 4, you pull 4 metres of rope to lift the load 1 metre."
  ],
